# TypeScript and OOP

Use strict TypeScript. Prefer `const`, async/await, explicit domain interfaces, and typed parameters.

Avoid `any`, `var`, unnecessary assertions, duplicated types, and magic strings/numbers. If `any` is unavoidable, comment why.

OOP: classes for pages, components, and flows; interfaces for domain models; `BasePage` as an abstract page base; constructor injection for Playwright `Page` and collaborators. Use `protected`/`private` for locators and helpers. Prefer composition unless inheritance expresses a real is-a page relationship.

```typescript
export abstract class BasePage {
  constructor(protected readonly page: Page) {}
}

export class LoginPage extends BasePage {
  private readonly email = this.page.getByLabel('Email');
  constructor(page: Page, private readonly header: Header) {
    super(page);
  }
}
```
